**Read:** [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), laminar and transitional flow; the shared pipe-flow and compressible-flow material. State the regime before using a vacuum conductance table.

For steady isothermal laminar flow of an ideal gas through a long circular tube, neglecting inertia and slip and taking viscosity constant, throughput Q=πr⁴(p₁²−p₂²)/(16μL). This follows by combining local Poiseuille flow with constant molar transfer along the tube.

Writing Q=C(p₁−p₂) then gives a conductance proportional to mean pressure. Molecular conductance under its usual assumptions is pressure-independent; the two laws describe different limits. Choking, entrance effects, heating, rarefaction or strong acceleration can invalidate this derivation.

**Work:** Derive the squared-pressure relation by integrating along the tube. Recover the incompressible small-pressure-difference limit. Check Reynolds and Knudsen measures using local states rather than only an upstream label. Explain why simply adding two limiting conductance formulas is not a universally valid transition model.

